std.debug — Assertions, panics, and fatal error reporting
===========================================================

The `std.debug` module provides the primitives used by all other
standard-library code to signal violated invariants, unreachable
code paths, and unfinished work. It is deliberately the lowest
layer of the standard library: it depends on nothing except the
runtime's trap mechanism.

All fatal paths here terminate the process (or the system, in
kernel contexts) via **trap 1**. Trap 1 is reserved throughout
the TRISC ecosystem as the *panic/fatal* trap. Its exact
runtime behavior is defined outside this module — the runtime
decides whether to print, log, halt, or reboot — but once trap 1
is taken, control never returns to the caller.

Contract summary:

- `panic(msg)` — report and terminate, never returns.
- `assert(cond, msg)` — panic if `cond` is false, otherwise no-op.
- `unreachable(msg)` — panic; marks code paths the programmer
  believes cannot be executed.
- `todo(msg)` — panic; marks code paths not yet implemented.

These four entry points are the only supported way to trigger
trap 1 from Sysl code.


Runtime interface
-----------------

`std.debug` relies on two primitives provided by the runtime:

- `__debug_write(msg: str)` — write a diagnostic message to the
  panic/console sink. MAY be unbuffered and MAY be a no-op in
  profiles that do not have a console (e.g. freestanding with
  no UART). This function MUST NOT fail and MUST NOT itself panic.
- `__debug_trap()` — issue trap 1. Never returns.

Both symbols are supplied by the platform runtime (slix, TOS,
or the hosted test harness). They are `extern` here; no Sysl
implementation exists in this module.


    module std.debug

    extern __debug_write(msg: str)
    extern __debug_trap()


panic
-----

`panic(msg)` reports `msg` to the debug sink and issues trap 1.
It never returns.

It is the single point through which all other `std.debug`
entry points funnel, and the only place in the standard library
that speaks directly to `__debug_trap`.

    panic(msg: str)
        __debug_write("panic: ")
        __debug_write(msg)
        __debug_write("\n")
        __debug_trap()


assert
------

`assert(cond, msg)` is a no-op when `cond` is true, and panics
with `msg` when `cond` is false. The message should describe
the invariant that was violated, not the condition that was
checked. Prefer:

> "buffer index out of range"

over:

> "i >= 0 && i < len"

Assertions are not stripped in any build profile. Sysl has no
release/debug distinction for assertions: if you do not want
the check at runtime, do not write it. This is a deliberate
choice — silently-disappearing checks are a common source of
bugs.

    assert(cond: bool, msg: str)
        if not cond then
            panic(msg)


unreachable
-----------

`unreachable(msg)` panics unconditionally. It marks code paths
the programmer asserts cannot execute — typically the default
arm of an exhaustive `match`, or the fall-through after a
non-returning call. The message should explain *why* the
programmer believes the path is unreachable.

    unreachable(msg: str)
        __debug_write("unreachable: ")
        __debug_write(msg)
        __debug_write("\n")
        __debug_trap()


todo
----

`todo(msg)` panics unconditionally. It marks code paths that
have not yet been implemented, so that partial code compiles
and links but fails loudly at runtime if exercised. The message
should describe what needs to be done.

`todo` is distinct from `unreachable` so that grep and tooling
can distinguish "not implemented yet" from "cannot happen".

    todo(msg: str)
        __debug_write("todo: ")
        __debug_write(msg)
        __debug_write("\n")
        __debug_trap()


Design notes
------------

**No source location capture yet.** Rust's `panic!`, `assert!`,
`unreachable!`, and `todo!` macros capture `file!()`/`line!()`
automatically. Sysl has no macro system, so location capture
would require dedicated compiler support (an intrinsic that
inserts a `SourceLoc` argument at the call site). This is
deferred; when added, it will be a non-breaking change — the
compiler will inject an extra argument that defaults to the
caller's location.

**No formatted messages.** These functions take a plain `str`,
not a format string. Formatting would require `std.builder` and
`std.strconv`, which depend on `std.debug` transitively through
the other `std` modules. Users who need a formatted panic
message should build the string themselves with `StrBuilder`
and pass the result to `panic`. This keeps `std.debug`
dependency-free.

**No recovery.** There is no `catch_panic` or unwinding. Trap 1
is terminal. Code that needs to fail recoverably should return
a `Result`, not panic.

**Kernel and userspace share this module.** The runtime
decides what trap 1 does — a hosted test harness may print to
stderr and `exit(1)`; slix may kill the current process; TOS
may halt the CPU. `std.debug` does not care.
